<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Philippine Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1943 CE – 1945 CE · capital Manila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Republic set up by Japan in occupied Philippines under Jose P. Laurel, whose legitimacy remains debated.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in January 1942, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> governed through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-executive-commission">Philippine Executive Commission</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-b-vargas">Jorge Vargas</a>. Seeking local support, it granted nominal independence, and on 14 October 1943 the Second <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-philippine-republic">Philippine Republic</a> was inaugurated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-p-laurel">Jose P. Laurel</a> as president under a new constitution.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-p-laurel">Laurel</a> tried to shield Filipinos from harsher Japanese demands and resisted conscription, but real power lay with the Japanese army, and food shortages and atrocities fuelled resentment. Widespread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance loyal to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commonwealth-of-the-philippines">Commonwealth of the Philippines</a> government in exile continued. In September 1944 the republic declared war on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As American forces returned in 1944 and 1945, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-p-laurel">Laurel</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and dissolved the republic on 17 August 1945. Its leaders faced collaboration charges but were amnestied in 1948, and Laurel is now counted among Philippine presidents.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-philippine-republic.jpg" alt="Second Philippine Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: User 50 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Philippines_(1943%E2%80%931945).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1943 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Manila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jose P. Laurel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tagalog, English, Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Japanese-sponsored Philippine Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=second-philippine-republic" aria-label="Second Philippine Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7989 1634 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Philippine Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8309 1890L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8309 1890h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Manila. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=second-philippine-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

